@using MUI.Web.Localization

@*
    The notice a machine-translated page carries, on every page, at the top.

    **◆ is the glyph that already means derived** — computed rather than observed — so a returning
    reader recognises the claim without learning a new symbol. Everything on this site says how it
    came to be known; this is the site applying that rule to its own words.

    **The second clause is load-bearing and is true.** "The measured values are unaffected" holds
    because the machine voice — game names, hostnames, codebase strings, versions, protocol acronyms,
    connect-screen output — carries `translate="no"` and never enters the pipeline at all. If that
    ever stopped being true, this sentence would become the most damaging one on the site, so it is
    asserted by a test rather than trusted.

    **And a way back.** A reader who would rather have words a person wrote can have them in one
    click, on the page they are already reading. It is the same address without the locale prefix,
    which is the canonical English one.

    Not dismissible. A reader who cannot tell a translated sentence from a written one is in exactly
    the position the demo banner exists to prevent them being in about the data.
*@

@if (Locale.IsMachineTranslated)
{
    <p class="mt-banner" role="status">
        <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">◆</span>
        <span>@Messages.For(Locale.Tag, "mt.banner")</span>
        <a href="@English">@Messages.For(Locale.Tag, "mt.original")</a>
    </p>
}

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    private Locale Locale => HttpContext.LocaleOf().Locale;

    /// <summary>
    /// This same page, unprefixed — which is the English one.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The querystring travels with it: a reader who has narrowed the listing to three facets and
    /// then asks for the original has not asked to start the listing again. The prefix is already
    /// off the path, because the middleware moved it into <c>PathBase</c>.
    /// </remarks>
    private string English =>
        HttpContext is { } context
            ? context.Request.Path.ToUriComponent() + context.Request.QueryString.ToUriComponent()
            : "/";
}
